<problem>
<!-- <title>critical points of absolute value</title> -->

<setup>

<function name="f" variables="x">abs(x+1)</function>
<math name="one">1</math>
<math name="n1">-1</math>
<subsetOfReals name="left_interval">(-infinity, $n1)</subsetOfReals>
<subsetOfReals name="right_interval">($n1, infinity)</subsetOfReals>
<math name="zero">0</math>

</setup>


<p>Let <m>f(x) = $f</m>, which is the same as the function
<me>f(x) = \begin{cases}
  x+1\amp \text{if $ x \gt -1 $ }\\
-x-1\amp \text{if  $ x \lt -1 $ }\\
0\amp \text{if $ x = -1 $ }.
\end{cases}</me></p>


<exercise>
Calculate <m>f'(x)</m>.  Be sure to note any points where the derivative does not exist.

<p><m>f'(x) =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$one </award>
</answer>
 if <m>x \gt</m> 
<answer>
<award>$n1 </award>
</answer>

</p>
<p>

<m>f'(x) =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$n1 </award>
</answer>
 if <m>x \lt</m> 
<answer>
<award>$n1 </award>
</answer>

</p>
<p>

<m>f'(x)</m> does not exist if <m>x=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$n1 </award>
</answer>
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
Find the critical points of <m>f</m>.

<p>Critical points: <m>x=</m> 
<answer>
<award>$n1 </award>
</answer>

</p>
<p>

Enter answers in increasing order, separated by commas.</p>


<hint>
Remember critical points are where the derivative is zero or does not exist.
</hint>
</exercise>

<exercise>
Determine the intervals over which <m>f</m> is increasing and decreasing.

<p>Left interval: <m>f</m> is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">decreasing</choice>
<choice>increasing</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
 on the interval 
<answer name="answer4">
  <mathInput name="mi4" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi4">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi4</subsetOfReals> = $left_interval</when>
  </award>
</answer>
</p>
  
<p>
Right interval: <m>f</m> is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">increasing</choice>
<choice>decreasing</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
 on the interval 
<answer name="answer6">
  <mathInput name="mi6" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi6">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi6</subsetOfReals> = $right_interval</when>
  </award>
</answer>
</p>


<hint>
You can write the intervals as <m>(a,b)</m> or as <m>a &lt; x &lt; b</m>.  Or if an interval extends to infinity, you can write it, for example, as <m>(-\infty, b)</m> or <m>x &lt; b</m>.
</hint>
</exercise>

<exercise>
Using this information and the value of <m>f(0) =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$one </award>
</answer>
, sketch the graph of <m>f(x)</m>.




<graph size="large" xmin="-5" xmax="5" ymin="-5" ymax="5" showNavigation="false" grid="true">

<point name="p1" x="-1" y="3">
<constrainToGrid />
</point>

<point name="p2" x="0" y="2">
<constrainToGrid />
</point>

<point name="p3" x="1" y="2">
<constrainToGrid />
</point>

<ray fixed="true" through="$p1" endpoint="$p2" />
<ray fixed="true" through="$p3" endpoint="$p2" />

</graph>


<setup>
<math name="px1" extend="$p1.x" />
<math name="px3" extend="$p3.x" />
<math name="py1" extend="$p1.y" />
<math name="py3" extend="$p3.y" />
</setup>

<p>
<answer>
<award><when>
$p2 = (-1,0) and

(( $px1 + 1 =  1 $py1 and $px1 > -1 and $px3 + 1 = -1 $py3 and $px3 &lt; -1 ) or 
 ( $px3 + 1 =  1 $py3 and $px3 > -1 and $px1 + 1 = -1 $py1 and $px1 &lt; -1 ))


</when></award>
</answer>
</p>
</exercise>


<solution>


<exercise>
<me>f'(x) = \begin{cases}
  1\amp \text{if $ x \gt  -1 $}\\
-1\amp \text{if $ x \lt  -1 $ }\\
\text{does not exist}\amp \text{if $ x=-1 $ }
\end{cases}</me>
</exercise>

<exercise>
Since <m>f'(x)</m> cannot be zero, the one critical point is at <m>x=-1</m>.
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>The intervals determined by the critical points are: <m>(-\infty, -1)</m> and <m>(-1,\infty)</m>.</p>

<p>Over the interval <m>(-\infty,-1)</m>, <m>f'(x)=-1 \lt  0</m> for all <m>x \in  (-\infty,-1)</m>.   <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing on the interval <m>(-\infty,-1)</m>.</p>

<p>For the interval <m>(-1,\infty)</m>, <m>f'(x)=1 \gt  0</m> for all <m>x \in (-1,\infty)</m>.  <m>f(x)</m> is increasing on the interval <m>(-1,\infty)</m>.</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<m>f(0) = 1</m>.

<graph size="large" xmin="-5" xmax="5" ymin="-5" ymax="5" showNavigation="false" grid="true">

<point fixed="true">(-1, 0)</point>
<ray fixed="true" endpoint="(-1, 0)" through="(-2,1)" />
<ray fixed="true" endpoint="(-1, 0)" through="(0,1)" />

</graph>

</exercise>

</solution>


</problem>